\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The center of an elementary abelian direction algebra}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Keep the unit-weight algebra #{A_n} of \ref{fdip-00QH}, so every
generator squares to #{1}. If #{2=0} in #{k}, then #{A_n} is
commutative. A basis expansion #{\sum_S c_S e_S} is central
if and only if #{2c_S=0} whenever #{S} is neither empty nor, for odd
#{n}, the full set. In particular, if #{2} is not a zerodivisor, the
center is the scalars when #{n} is even, and the free module on #{1}
and the volume monomial when #{n} is odd. In the odd case the volume
squares to #{(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}}. For #{n=1} and #{2} a unit,
#{A_1\cong k\times k}. For #{n=2} and #{2} a unit,
#{A_2\cong M_2(k)}, so the commutant of the standard module is
#{k}. The Brauer class for even #{n>2} is left open.}

\proof{
\p{A basis monomial #{e_S} commutes past a generator #{e_j} with
sign #{(-1)^{|S|}} when #{j\notin S}, and with sign
#{(-1)^{|S|-1}} when #{j\in S}. The second sign is the count of
the remaining factors after #{e_j} is moved to the front. For a
coefficient #{c_S}, the two transports agree if and only if
#{c_S} equals that sign times #{c_S}. The sign is #{-1} for some
generator precisely when #{S} is neither empty nor, for odd #{n},
the full set, and then the condition is #{2c_S=0}. If #{2=0}, every
coefficient is free and the algebra is commutative. If #{2} is not
a zerodivisor, those coefficients vanish. The full monomial is then
central only for odd #{n}, and its square is the reordering sign
#{(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}}.}

\p{For #{n=1} the idempotents #{(1\pm u)/2} are complementary.
For #{n=2} the matrices
##{e\mapsto\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
f\mapsto\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}}
obey the same relations, and the #{4} matrices are a basis of
#{M_2(k)} once #{2} is a unit. The homomorphism is an
isomorphism. The commutant of #{k^2} in that matrix algebra is the
scalar matrices.}
}
